---
title: Japanese milk bread
servings: 10
yield: 1 loaf, 9 x 5 inches
tags: [japanese, vegetarian]
cuisine: japanese
time: 3 hours 25 minutes
source: Japanese Milk Bread by Charlotte Rutledge, King Arthur Baking, https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/japanese-milk-bread-recipe
---

== Tangzhong ==

Whisk the @water{43%g}, @whole milk{43%g} and @bread flour{14%g} in a
#small saucepan{} until no lumps are left.

Cook over low heat, whisking all the time, ~{3%minutes} to 5 minutes, until
thick. Scrape it into a bowl and let it cool to lukewarm.

== Dough ==

Put the tangzhong in the bowl of a #stand mixer{} with the
@bread flour{300%g}, @nonfat dry milk{14%g}, @granulated sugar{50%g},
@table salt{6%g}, @instant yeast{1%tbsp}, @whole milk{113%g}, @eggs{1} and
@unsalted butter{57%g}(melted).

Mix, then knead until smooth and elastic. In a mixer this can take nearly
~{15%minutes}.

Shape it into a ball, set it in a lightly greased bowl, cover and leave
~{1%hour} to 1 1/2 hours, until puffy.

== Shape and bake ==

Grease a #9x5-inch loaf pan{}. Deflate the dough and divide it into four equal
pieces, 170 to 175 g each.

Flatten one piece into a 5 x 8-inch rectangle. Fold the short ends in to meet,
flatten it to about 3 x 6 inches, then roll it up from a short end into a
4-inch log. Repeat with the rest.

Lay the four logs seam down, side by side, in the pan. Cover and leave
~{40%minutes} to 50 minutes, until puffy. Heat the oven to 350°F (175°C) near
the end of the rise.

Brush the top with @whole milk{}. Bake ~{30%minutes} to 35 minutes, until
golden brown and the middle reads at least 190°F (88°C).

Cool in the pan until you can handle it, then turn it out onto a
#wire rack{} to cool completely.

> Well wrapped, it stays soft 5 to 7 days at cool room temperature. Freeze it
> for longer.
